# Target C-II — every all-inactive two-sided FREE ancestry is outer-face localized

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting promotion; proof-only strengthening of two-sided inactive gate absorption  
**Scope:** non-anchored FREE reflection modules, all-inactive two-sided activation ancestry  
**Dependencies:** C65 full diameter visibility; `F=conv B`; FREE blocker force-hull exclusion; two-sided inactive gate absorption; inactive-chain contact-crossing threshold  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_two_sided_outer_face_localization_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The two-sided absorption theorem allowed an inactive upper gate to jump between contact faces, so its source-wide lower-gate matching was not previously localized. The upper-gate child, however, cannot lie in the force hull unless an active diameter endpoint becomes tight at the same instant. This removes the face-jump ambiguity on the vertices themselves.

Let

\[
F=\operatorname{conv}B,
\]

where `B` is the complete set of actual source-diameter endpoints supplied by C65. In an all-inactive two-sided activation branch, every vertex of the activation ancestry lies in the unbounded face of the original positive contact graph. More precisely:

1. every blocker root lies outside `F`;
2. every inactive child born at an **upper** gate lies outside `F`;
3. every **lower** gate preserves the current contact face because its original segment has length below `sqrt(3)` and cannot cross the unit-contact graph.

Hence every maximal lower-gate run lies in the original outer contact face, and every ancestry vertex is an outer-face isolated source point. Upper-gate segments themselves may cross the contact core; no claim is made that the full polygonal ancestry is embedded in the outer face.

This upgrades both branches of the two-boundary slack tradeoff:

- the upper-rich matching consists of old fully inactive pairs whose **endpoints are both outer-face vertices**;
- the upper-poor deep run from the preceding theorem is **always an outer-face run**, never a bounded exceptional-face run.

It also localizes the source-wide inverse-`D` lower matching from two-sided absorption. For every fixed `K>h_*`, where

\[
h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52},
\]

a non-anchored FREE module in the all-inactive branch forces a vertex-disjoint matching of outer-face isolated source points of size

\[
\boxed{
\left({1\over2}-{h_*\over2K}-o(1)\right)D
}
\]

with

\[
\boxed{1<|x-y|\le1+{K\over D}.}
\]

For `K=1`, the coefficient remains

\[
0.3326260078314499\ldots,
\]

but the matching is now entirely localized to the old outer contact face.

---

## 1. Active diameter endpoints stay fixed throughout the all-inactive repair

The two-sided activation process moves only blocker roots and old source vertices that were fully inactive in the original source. If a moving vertex first reaches an old active contact/diameter vertex at either metric boundary, the process stops in the `ACTIVE-GATE / exact-anchor` branch.

Consequently, on an all-inactive ancestry, every vertex of

\[
B
\]

remains fixed at its original position throughout the activation process.

At every pre-stop time the deformed configuration still satisfies the upper metric inequalities

\[
|p-b|\le D
\qquad(b\in B)
\tag{OF2.1}
\]

for every moving point `p`. If equality with some `b in B` occurs, that is exactly an active upper gate and the all-inactive branch terminates.

---

## 2. An inactive upper-gate child cannot lie in the force hull

Consider an upper ancestry edge. Immediately before the child activates, let `p` be the current position of the already-moving parent and let `v` be the still-fixed old-inactive child. At the first upper hit,

\[
|p-v|=D.
\tag{OF2.2}
\]

Assume for contradiction that

\[
v\in F=\operatorname{conv}B.
\]

Write

\[
v=\sum_{b\in B}\mu_b b,
\qquad
\mu_b\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_b\mu_b=1.
\]

By convexity of the Euclidean norm,

\[
D=|p-v|
\le
\sum_b\mu_b|p-b|
\le
\max_{b\in B}|p-b|.
\tag{OF2.3}
\]

On the other hand, upper safety (OF2.1) gives

\[
\max_{b\in B}|p-b|\le D.
\]

Thus equality holds and there is at least one

\[
b\in B
\]

such that

\[
|p-b|=D.
\tag{OF2.4}
\]

But `b` is an old active actual-diameter endpoint and remains fixed. Equation (OF2.4) is therefore a simultaneous active upper gate. The process must stop in the exact-anchor branch instead of activating `v` as an all-inactive child, contradiction.

Hence every upper-gate child on an all-inactive ancestry satisfies

\[
\boxed{v\notin F.}
\tag{OF2.5}
\]

Since the positive contact graph lies in `F`, every point outside `F` lies in its unbounded face. Therefore every inactive upper-gate child is an outer-face source point.

This argument is exact. It does not use small gate slack, the support direction, or an asymptotic estimate.

---

## 3. Lower gates preserve the outer face

Take a lower ancestry edge between original source points `u,v`. The two-sided gate theorem gives

\[
1<|u-v|
\le1+\Delta
\le1+t_D
<1+h_*+o(1)
<\sqrt3.
\tag{OF2.6}
\]

The sharp contact-crossing lemma says that a source segment crossing a unit-contact edge has length at least `sqrt(3)`. A segment of length below two also cannot pass through a third source point because the source is unit-separated.

Thus the straight segment `uv` does not cross the original positive contact graph and does not pass through one of its vertices. Its endpoints therefore lie in the same face of that graph.

In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\text{if }u\text{ lies in the outer contact face and }uv\text{ is a lower gate, then }v\text{ also lies in the outer contact face.}
}
\tag{OF2.7}

---

## 4. Induction along the complete ancestry

Let

\[
z_0,z_1,\ldots,z_m
\]

be an all-inactive ancestry from a FREE blocker root.

The FREE blocker theorem gives

\[
z_0\notin F,
\]

so `z_0` lies in the outer contact face.

Assume `z_j` is an outer-face vertex.

- If `z_jz_{j+1}` is a lower gate, (OF2.7) keeps `z_{j+1}` in the same outer face.
- If it is an upper gate, (OF2.5) gives `z_{j+1} notin F`, hence `z_{j+1}` lies in the outer face independently of where the upper segment travels.

Induction yields

\[
\boxed{
z_0,\ldots,z_m\text{ are all old fully inactive vertices in the original outer contact face.}
}
\tag{OF2.8}

Every maximal consecutive lower run is therefore a simple path entirely contained in that same outer face.

The qualification about the edges matters: an upper-gate chord can cross the contact graph. The theorem localizes the source vertices and all lower-gate runs, not the interiors of upper-gate chords.

---

## 5. Consequences for the two-boundary slack tradeoff

The two-boundary theorem has a common defect ledger

\[
\sum_{j\in E_{\rm low}}(|z_j-z_{j+1}|-1)
+
\sum_{j\in E_{\rm up}}(D-|z_j-z_{j+1}|)
\le t_D.
\tag{OF2.9}
\]

All pairs in both sums now have outer-face endpoints by (OF2.8).

### Upper-rich branch

If `U>=M`, the existing parity thinning gives a vertex-disjoint matching of size

\[
{M\over4}-O(1)
\]

with

\[
D-{2t_D\over M}
\le |x-y|<D.
\]

The new conclusion is

\[
\boxed{
\text{every endpoint of this near-diameter matching is an old fully inactive outer-face vertex.}
}
\tag{OF2.10}

The matching chords themselves need not remain in the outer face.

### Upper-poor branch

If `U<M`, the support-progress pigeonhole chooses one consecutive all-lower run `R` with

\[
\operatorname{span}_N(R)
\ge
{D-O(\sqrt D)\over M}.
\]

The preceding upper-poor theorem allowed `R` to lie in a bounded exceptional face or in the outer face. Equation (OF2.8) removes the former alternative:

\[
\boxed{R\text{ lies in the original outer contact face.}}
\tag{OF2.11}
\]

At the reflection-matched scale `M=floor(D^(1/4))`, one therefore gets an **outer-face** simple inactive run with

\[
\operatorname{span}_N(R)
\ge D^{3/4}-O(D^{1/4}),
\]

and an outer-face vertex-disjoint matching of

\[
\left({1\over4}-o(1)\right)D^{3/4}
\]

pairs with

\[
|x-y|-1
\le
(0.6694959686742\ldots+o(1))D^{-3/4}.
\tag{OF2.12}
\]

There is no bounded exceptional-face escape at this scale.

---

## 6. Source-wide inverse-diameter matching is also outer-face localized

The two-sided activation theorem gives at least

\[
D-O(\sqrt D)
\]

lower gates in any ancestry reaching the near-opposite threshold. Their total original unit excess is at most `t_D<h_*+o(1)`.

For every fixed `K>h_*`, after deleting the lower edges with excess above `K/D` and parity thinning, the matching size is

\[
\left({1\over2}-{h_*\over2K}-o(1)\right)D.
\]

By (OF2.8), every endpoint of every retained lower edge lies in the outer contact face. Hence

\[
\boxed{
\left({1\over2}-{h_*\over2K}-o(1)\right)D
\text{ disjoint outer-face inactive pairs satisfy }
1<|x-y|\le1+K/D.
}
\tag{OF2.13}
\]

For `K=1`,

\[
\boxed{
(0.3326260078314499\ldots-o(1))D
}
\]

such pairs are forced.

For any `K_D->infinity` with `K_D=o(D)`, the outer face contains a matching of

\[
\boxed{(1/2-o(1))D}
\]

fully inactive pairs of length `1+o(1)`.

This converts the previously source-wide two-sided near-unit burden into the exact KKT-invisible region already singled out by the outer-pocket/Oler and outer-detour parts of Target C.

---

## 7. Remaining seam

The all-inactive two-sided branch can no longer hide lower-gate mass in bounded exceptional faces. Every lower run, including the support-deep upper-poor run, is in the old outer contact face, and every upper-rich near-diameter witness also has outer-face endpoints.

A closure still needs one more geometric step: charge the resulting outer-face inactive population/run against either

1. the internal outer-pocket area/detour inside `F`, or
2. the external population outside `F`,

without double-counting the same outer-walk currency already used by the canonical Target C ledger.

**Disposition:** proved force-hull exclusion for every inactive upper-gate child and used it to localize the complete all-inactive two-sided ancestry, all lower-gate runs, the source-wide inverse-`D` lower matching, and both branches of the two-boundary tradeoff to the original outer contact face. C-II and Level I remain open.
